vo driver would not be reached at the documented address.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A0\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A1\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40\"}], \"impact\": \"The FCC firmware addressing the PWM driver at 0x40 gets no response, so servo/PWM output fails. Only the address changes; the rest of the wiring is unaffected.\", \"confidence\": 0.45}, {\"category\": \"open_connection\", \"components\": [\"FCC1\", \"SW1\", \"C31\"], \"pins\": [\"FCC1.14\"], \"nets\": [\"unconnected-(FCC1-NRST-Pad14)\", \"NRST_FCC\"], \"observed\": \"The FCC1 NRST pin (pin 14) sits on its own unconnected net. The reset net NRST_FCC, which carries the reset switch SW1, the reset capacitor C31 and the SWD header, does not include the MCU reset pin. The NAVC1 NRST pin shows the same pattern.\", \"expected\": \"The MCU NRST pin should be on the NRST_FCC net so that the reset switch and SWD reset line actually reset the processor.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"174\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(FCC1-NRST-Pad14)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"FCC1\\\") (pin \\\"14\\\") (pinfunction \\\"NRST\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\")))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"61\\\") (name \\\"NRST_FCC\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"C31\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")) (node (ref \\\"SW1\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") ...)\"}], \"impact\": \"If the pin is truly unconnected, the reset switch and SWD reset do nothing to the FCC. The same pattern on NAVC1 is systematic, so it may be a reference-design artifact rather than tampering. Confidence is low.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}], \"summary\": \"The most likely anomaly is LED5 being installed with its anode and cathode reversed relative to every other indicator LED, so the MCU drives the cathode. A weaker concern is the PCA9685 A0 pin tied high, which shifts its address away from the specified 0x40.\"}",
